## 3. Test expectation updated (the one change outside the content scope, permitted by the WO)
src/data/__tests__/guidance-disclosure-parity.test.ts:313
  -  nb: /rådgivningssteget/,
  +  nb: /rådgivningsmøte/,
Reason: the row a#37 wording "denne siden er ikke rådgivningssteget" is exactly the German coinage the
reviewer flagged; the test pinned the old string. Nothing else in tests was touched.
(Two other near-misses were resolved WITHOUT touching tests: the "not-an-appointment" element pins
/\btime\b/, and "time" is in fact the idiomatic Bokmål word for an appointment, so I reverted my own
"møtetid" edit; and the "english" structural check twice flagged a rewritten sentence that had become
Latin-only with ≥3 English stopwords — both were rephrased, keeping a Chinese gloss, not test-edited.)

## 4. Checker constraints I had to respect (noted per the WO)
- Thousands separator: a#49 and b#17 ask for space ("500 000", "25 000 000"). The checker parses nb
  with NUMBER_THOUSAND_STYLE 'dot'; "25 000 000 TWD" tokenizes as 25 / 0 / 0, i.e. the source amount goes
  MISSING and `numbers` FAILs. Verified with extractNormalizedNumbers before deciding. Dots therefore
  kept in the columns (TWD 500.000, TWD 25.000.000, 1.570.000 …), as ACCEPTED-nb allows ("else keep").
  The guidance pack is not covered by the checker, so a#42 there uses the Norwegian form "1 570 000 TWD".
- Spelled-out numerals: "ett"/"én"/"tjue" are NOT in the checker's nb word-numeral lexicon, so
  "1 år" → "ett år" or "20 lastebiler" → "tjue lastebiler" would drop a source token and FAIL. Digits kept
  for those. Words that ARE in the lexicon (to, tre, fem, seks, ti, tolv) and the R11 lexical items
  (tredjemann, første/andre instans) were spelled out.
- b#38 "rang 1" → "1. arveklasse" rather than "første arveklasse", for the same reason (제1순위 in the
  source carries a 1 that is not stripped by R11).
- ROC-year glosses (a#59 / b#18) add a year that is not in the Korean source, so 003/007/010 now show
  `numbers  WARN  extra in translation`. WARN does not fail the checker; summary is PASS 18/18.
  Files with a numbers WARN after the fix: 003 (2020×2, 2023), 006 (1×2), 007 (2023×4), 010, 018.

## 5. Reviewer rows I did NOT apply (and why)
- b#32, guillemet half ("French/German guillemets « » in 013, 015, 017"): NOT applied. RULEBOOK-FIX R6
  explicitly lists nb among the locales that DO use « », and Norwegian typography uses «…» as its primary
  quotation marks. Changing them would have made the files less native, not more. The "**Q.**" half of
  the row was applied.
- a#15 / b#9 (Hangul-slug wei-wei-lawyer.com link in 005; Invest Taiwan `lang=jpn` link in 011): skipped
  per ACCEPTED-nb and the WO hard rule that external links stay byte-identical. Both are still live in
  the files; they need a supervisor decision, not a translator edit.
- a#5's second half ("For norske mottakere gjelder i utgangspunktet intern taiwansk kildeskatt (vanligvis
  21 %)"): NOT applied as a Norway-specific statement. ACCEPTED/R2 forbid adding claims about the reader's
  own country. I used the neutral R2b framing instead ("Om det finnes en skatteavtale mellom Taiwan og ditt
  eget land … må undersøkes særskilt"). The 21 % internal rate already stands in 001 §5 as a Taiwan fact.
- a#8's 贍養費 gloss: ACCEPTED proposed 'ektefellebidrag'; I used "underhold etter skilsmisse (贍養費)",
  which is the term Norwegian family lawyers use for post-divorce maintenance and matches the body text
  the reviewer himself praised. Flagging it as a deliberate deviation.
- a#53 / b#1 suggested inserting a `[Kontakt](/nb/contact)` link. NOT done: the `links` check compares
  href counts against the Korean source, and those spots have no link in the source. The sentences point
  to the contact page in prose instead.
- 018 b#44 offered "delete the stray sentence". I kept a sentence (rewritten) rather than deleting, because
  deleting would change the block count the checker compares.

## 6. Residual issues a human should look at (not fixable inside this WO)
- The conservative "definite article + indefinite noun" pattern ("det taiwanske datterselskap", "den
  taiwanske filial") is still pervasive in 001/002/004/007/014/016/017. It is attested formal Bokmål, it
  was not on any accepted row, and normalizing it is a full re-translation rather than a token sweep.
- 008 and 009 frontmatter `url:` values are raw Hangul (canonical source metadata) — untouched by rule.
- 011 links to Invest Taiwan with `lang=jpn`; 005 links to a percent-encoded Hangul blog slug (see §5).

